[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which part of the app should the logging cover? → Both, shared module[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Both, shared module — so the logger has to work in a browser and in Node without branching at its call sites.

  Next question: how browser logs actually reach you. This is the decision that's expensive to reverse.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether logs leave the user's machine, and if so, to what.

  - [1mConsole only, with levels + a level switch[0m — the logger formats and filters; output stays in devtools. Buys you
    consistency and the ability to turn debug on in production without a redeploy (via a [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m or query-param
    flag). Costs: you only see logs when you can ask the user to open devtools and copy them. No new endpoint, no
    privacy surface, nothing to undo.
  - [1mConsole + in-memory ring buffer[0m — same, plus the last N entries retained and dumped on demand ([38;5;153mwindow.__logs()[39m, or
    attached to an error report). Buys you the history leading up to a failure, which is usually the thing you actually
    need. Still no network, still nothing to undo. Small extra cost: buffer size and memory bound.
  - [1mConsole + POST to a collector[0m — logs ship to an endpoint automatically. Buys you real remote debugging. Costs the
    most: you need an endpoint that doesn't exist yet, a batching/retry policy, a failure mode when the collector is
    down, and a privacy review — this app handles passwords. This is the one that's hard to undo, because the endpoint
    becomes an interface and the shipped data becomes a retention question.

  [1mMy recommendation: console + ring buffer.[0m It gives you the pre-failure history that makes production bugs debuggable,
  needs no backend this repo doesn't have, and the buffer is exactly the seam a collector would plug into later if you
  decide you want one.
